# .gitleaksignore — exact-fingerprint exemptions for findings that live
# ONLY in immutable git history.
#
# Why this file exists alongside .gitleaks.toml's [[rules.allowlists]]:
# those entries are `paths`-based, so they exempt an entire file for all
# time — correct for the decoder-fixture tests, whose whole point is
# holding high-entropy public XDR blobs. This file is for the other
# shape: a one-off string that tripped the heuristic in a PAST commit and
# has already been removed from the working tree. A path allowlist would
# be far too wide for that — it would permanently blind the scanner to a
# live file over a string that is no longer even in it.
#
# A fingerprint is `commit:path:rule:line`, so each line here exempts
# exactly one finding in exactly one historical commit. If the same
# string reappears in a NEW commit, the fingerprint differs and gitleaks
# fails — which is the behaviour we want.
#
# Bar for adding a line: the string must be provably not a credential
# (and if it ever WAS one, the answer is rotation, not an ignore entry).
# Say which, and why, next to it.

# A fake Redis usage-counter subject key in a unit-test fixture, added by
# 6721eacb and removed in cacf6731 (replaced with an inert literal so the
# scanner stops seeing it going forward). `kid_515c8d94191f4e93` is a
# hand-typed identifier for a table-driven test of the usage-rollup
# backfill's key parsing — it never authenticated anything and matches no
# key this system issues. gitleaks flags it purely on Shannon entropy
# (3.55) of the hex-looking tail. Kept as a fingerprint rather than a
# path allowlist so usage_rollup_backfill_test.go stays scanned.
